<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michel Aflaq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michel Aflaq was a Syrian philosopher, sociologist and Arab nationalist, considered by several Ba'athists to be the principal founder of Ba'athist thought. Born into a middle-class <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, he studied at the Sorbonne, and in 1940 founded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Salah al-Din</a> al-Bitar the Arab Ihya Movement, which merged with Zaki al-Arsuzi's group in 1947 to form the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baathism">Ba'athism</a> held that the Arab world must be unified into one Arab Nation, and joined Arab socialism to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secularism">secularism</a>. Ousted in the 1966 Syrian coup d'état, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, where he was Secretary General of the Iraqi-led Ba'ath Party from 1968 until his death in 1989, without de facto power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq.jpg" alt="Michel Aflaq" width="330" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basch, Fritz · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michel_Aflaq_1963.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michel Aflaq was born on 9 January 1910 to an Orthodox Christian family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, where his father Joseph worked as a grain merchant. He was educated in the Westernised schools of the French Mandate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. In 1929 he went to the Sorbonne to study philosophy, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-bergson">Henri Bergson</a>'s works influenced him and he met his long-term collaborator Salah al-Din al-Bitar. He founded an Arab Student Union there and discovered the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>. Back in Syria in 1932 he became active in communist politics, but he left the movement when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s Popular Front government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-blum">Léon Blum</a> kept up the old colonial policies and the Syrian-Lebanese Communist Party backed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Aflaq and al-Bitar taught at the Tajhiz secondary school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Aflaq history and al-Bitar mathematics and physics. In 1940 they founded the Arab Ihya Movement, renamed the Arab Ba'ath Movement in 1942. The name led Zaki al-Arsuzi, leader of a group with the same name, to accuse them of taking it, and the two men became bitter rivals. At the first congress of the Arab Ba'ath Party at Damascus in 1947, which merged the two groups, Aflaq became Amid, the party's leader, with responsibility for ideology.</p><p>The party merged with Akram al-Hawrani's Arab Socialist Party in 1952, and Aflaq was elected its Secretary General in 1954. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> pressed for the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Aflaq dissolved the party without consulting its members. He was forced to resign as leader in 1965 in a struggle with the party's Military Committee. A coup on 23 February 1966, led by Salah Jadid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a>, overthrew the Syrian government and the party leadership and exiled him. He escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In 1968 he was elected Secretary General of the Iraqi-led Ba'ath Party, but he held no de facto power. The Syrian-led party sentenced him to death in absentia in 1971.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Aflaq's books include The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, The Battle for One Destiny and The Struggle Against Distorting the Movement of Arab Revolution. He held that the Arab world must be unified into one Arab nation to achieve development, and that freedom would come to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> through unity. He criticised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> alike, and held that Arab socialism was not part of the international socialist movement. Aflaq favoured separating state and religion, and opposed atheism. He argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the most sublime expression of Arabism, a view that drew criticism from devout Muslims and from some Christians. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 23 June 1989 and was given a state funeral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michel Aflaq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Michel Aflaq</b>, a Christian, called the rise of Islam in the seventh century the first Arab renaissance; when he died in 1989 the Iraqi Regional Command announced that he had converted to Islam, but his family was said not to know of any conversion.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/baathism">Ba'athism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A more formal ideology was set out in the 1930s by Syrian thinkers: Constantin Zureiq, Sati’ al-Husri, Zaki al-Arsuzi and <b>Michel Aflaq</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Michel Aflaq</b>, principal founder of Ba’athism, coined Arab socialism, joining socialism to Arab nationalism, and his thought was carried further in Syria, Egypt and Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in 5th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1961" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5477 948 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michel Aflaq"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5927 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1961">What was here in 1961? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
